item.content:encoded:
Ahead of the Global AI Forum for Humanity (GAFH) 2026, to be held in Seoul on June 12, Cheonji Ilbo is featuring a relay interview series with key speakers. Under the theme &ldquo;Trustworthy AI for the Common Good of Humanity,&rdquo; the forum will explore human-centered values, ethics, and global governance. Through insights from experts across fields, it aims to examine the direction of the AI era and envision a future that goes beyond technology to serve humanity and society.



<img alt="[Cheonji Ilbo = Reporter Nam Seungwoo] Choi Eunsoo, CEO of Intellibix, is being interviewed by Cheonji Ilbo at his office. Choi currently serves as CEO of Intellibix, a chaired professor of AI at Seoul School of Integrated Sciences and Technologies (aSSIST), vice president of the AI Management Society, and a judge for the CES Innovation Awards. ⓒ Cheonji Ilbo 2026.05.01." src="https://www.newscj.com/news/photo/202605/3398098_3495512_1123.jpg" />
[Cheonji Ilbo = Reporter Nam Seungwoo] Choi Eunsoo, CEO of Intellibix, is being interviewed by Cheonji Ilbo at his office. Choi currently serves as CEO of Intellibix, a chaired professor of AI at Seoul School of Integrated Sciences and Technologies (aSSIST), vice president of the AI Management Society, and a judge for the CES Innovation Awards. ⓒ Cheonji Ilbo 2026.05.01.



[Cheonji Ilbo=Reporter Kim Jeongpil]&nbsp;&ldquo;Artificial intelligence is no longer just a tool that analyzes and informs. It has become a technology that acts. At that point, &lsquo;trust&rsquo; is no longer optional&mdash;it is a prerequisite for entering industry.&rdquo;

Choi Eunsoo, the CEO Intellibix,&nbsp;who will deliver a keynote in Session 3 of GAFH 2026, summarized the shift in AI with this statement. In the past, AI functioned as a tool that analyzed data and suggested outcomes. Today, it has evolved into an &ldquo;acting technology&rdquo; that makes decisions and executes them.

&ldquo;The moment AI begins to intervene in real-world decision-making and action, trust is no longer an ethical value but a condition for industry,&rdquo; he said. &ldquo;Without controllability and a clear accountability structure, companies and industries cannot adopt AI.&rdquo;


◆ &ldquo;Edge Cases&rdquo; Are Where Accidents Begin


This shift is not simply technological advancement. It represents a structural transformation of industry.

For instance, while traditional AI might detect a fire and issue an alert, modern AI can analyze the location, choose an appropriate response, and decide whether to use water or gas, or which assets to protect first.

&ldquo;AI has moved beyond recommending results to becoming an entity that produces them,&rdquo; Choi said. &ldquo;From that moment, what matters is not how smart it is, but how trustworthy it is.&rdquo;


<img alt="[Cheonji Ilbo = Reporter Nam Seungwoo] Choi Eunsoo, CEO of Intellibix, is being interviewed by Cheonji Ilbo at his office. Choi said, “AI has now gone beyond recommending results to becoming an entity that creates outcomes,” adding, “From this moment on, what matters far more than ‘how smart it is’ is ‘how trustworthy it is.’” ⓒ Cheonji Ilbo 2026.05.01." src="https://www.newscj.com/news/photo/202605/3398098_3495513_1314.jpg" />
[Cheonji Ilbo = Reporter Nam Seungwoo] Choi Eunsoo, CEO of Intellibix, is being interviewed by Cheonji Ilbo at his office. Choi said, &ldquo;AI has now gone beyond recommending results to becoming an entity that creates outcomes,&rdquo; adding, &ldquo;From this moment on, what matters far more than &lsquo;how smart it is&rsquo; is &lsquo;how trustworthy it is.&rsquo;&rdquo; ⓒ Cheonji Ilbo 2026.05.01.



He cautioned against the illusion of &ldquo;95% accuracy.&rdquo; While many companies emphasize high accuracy rates, in industrial settings, the remaining 5% can lead to accidents.

&ldquo;Performance in a lab and trust in the field are entirely different,&rdquo; he said. &ldquo;Trust is determined not by average accuracy, but by how failures are handled.&rdquo;

In real industrial environments, countless variables exist&mdash;lighting, weather, equipment condition, work methods. These create &ldquo;edge cases&rdquo; for AI, and such exceptions often become the starting point of accidents.

For this reason, he emphasized &ldquo;domain-specific AI.&rdquo; General-purpose AI cannot handle the complexity of industrial environments. Medical AI must learn medical data, manufacturing AI must learn process data, and security AI must learn risk-specific data.

&ldquo;Just as a doctor cannot perform surgery without medical knowledge, AI cannot manage real-world operations without domain knowledge,&rdquo; he explained.

However, in Korea, industrial data is often locked within companies, and data-sharing structures remain weak.

&ldquo;Trustworthy AI ultimately starts with data, but this is where we are blocked,&rdquo; he said. &ldquo;A national-level framework for data utilization and compensation must be established.&rdquo;


<img alt="[Cheonji Ilbo = Reporter Nam Seungwoo] Choi Eunsoo, CEO of Intellibix, is being interviewed by Cheonji Ilbo at his office. ⓒ Cheonji Ilbo 2026.05.01." src="https://www.newscj.com/news/photo/202605/3398098_3495514_1428.jpg" />
[Cheonji Ilbo = Reporter Nam Seungwoo] Choi Eunsoo, CEO of Intellibix, is being interviewed by Cheonji Ilbo at his office. ⓒ Cheonji Ilbo 2026.05.01.




◆ &ldquo;AI Decisions Must Be Verified by Other AI and Humans&rdquo;


According to Choi, trust must be secured not through reactive measures but through proactive design.

Comparing AI to a building, he said, &ldquo;You don&rsquo;t fix cracks after completion&mdash;you design safety into the structure from the beginning.&rdquo;

He outlined essential elements such as:

risk classification, false-positive scenario training, pre-deployment validation, decision logging, and post-incident feedback systems.

He also introduced the concept of a &ldquo;dual verification structure,&rdquo; where AI decisions are validated by another AI and by humans.

&ldquo;In a system where AI is blindly trusted, trust can never emerge,&rdquo; he said. &ldquo;Verification systems are the core of trust.&rdquo;

At the center of AI control remains the human. However, he stressed that human involvement should not be reduced to a final approval step. Instead, a multilayered system combining technical mechanisms, operational structures, and institutional frameworks is required.

For example, routine alerts can be automated, but irreversible decisions&mdash;such as halting production or blocking access&mdash;must involve human oversight.

&ldquo;Controllable AI is not the result of a good model, but of a well-designed authority structure,&rdquo; he said. &ldquo;Human-in-the-loop is not optional&mdash;it is essential.&rdquo;


<img alt="[Cheonji Ilbo = Reporter Nam Seungwoo] Choi Eunsoo, CEO of Intellibix, explains a system powered by his company’s AI technology after the interview with Cheonji Ilbo at his office. ⓒ Cheonji Ilbo 2026.05.01." src="https://www.newscj.com/news/photo/202605/3398098_3495515_1539.jpg" />
[Cheonji Ilbo = Reporter Nam Seungwoo] Choi Eunsoo, CEO of Intellibix, explains a system powered by his company&rsquo;s AI technology after the interview with Cheonji Ilbo at his office. ⓒ Cheonji Ilbo 2026.05.01.




◆ Breaking Dependence&hellip; Korea Must Build &ldquo;AI Sovereignty&rdquo;


Choi identified &ldquo;plausible errors&rdquo; as the greatest risk of AI. Systems that partially fail are more dangerous than those that completely stop, because such errors are harder to detect and accumulate over time, eventually leading to major incidents.

&ldquo;The moment you trust AI 100%, human judgment stops,&rdquo; he warned. &ldquo;Risk management must always assume uncertainty.&rdquo;

Responsibility must also be addressed accordingly.

&ldquo;It is no longer acceptable to say &lsquo;the AI did it&rsquo; to avoid accountability,&rdquo; he said. Responsibility must be distributed across design, development, deployment, and operation. Companies must implement and demonstrate technical and institutional safeguards against foreseeable risks. This is not just a legal obligation, but a prerequisite for industrial trust.

Globally, evaluation criteria are already shifting. As a judge for the CES Innovation Awards since 2025, Choi noted that the benchmark for AI competition has changed.

&ldquo;In the past, model size and performance were key. Now stability, transparency, explainability, and accountability are evaluated together,&rdquo; he said. &ldquo;What matters is not how advanced the technology is, but how reliably it works in real-world environments.&rdquo;

Amid these changes, he proposed &ldquo;AI sovereignty&rdquo; as the direction for Korea&rsquo;s AI industry.

&ldquo;Dependence on foreign platforms makes it difficult to secure both industrial competitiveness and national security,&rdquo; he said. &ldquo;Korea has strengths in manufacturing, semiconductors, and urban infrastructure, but has yet to connect them through data and certification systems. We must move beyond exporting technology to exporting standards of trust.&rdquo;


<img alt="[Cheonji Ilbo = Reporter Nam Seungwoo] Choi Eunsoo, CEO of Intellibix, poses for a commemorative photo after the interview with Cheonji Ilbo at his office. ⓒ Cheonji Ilbo 2026.05.01." src="https://www.newscj.com/news/photo/202605/3398098_3495516_1623.jpg" />
[Cheonji Ilbo = Reporter Nam Seungwoo] Choi Eunsoo, CEO of Intellibix, poses for a commemorative photo after the interview with Cheonji Ilbo at his office. ⓒ Cheonji Ilbo 2026.05.01.



Choi likened AI to electricity. Just as electricity is a national infrastructure, AI must be built and managed as a foundational system by the state.

&ldquo;If not everyone has access to AI, the knowledge gap will only widen,&rdquo; he said. &ldquo;Korea must build a national large language model that anyone can use.&rdquo;

For companies, he recommended an &ldquo;AI-native strategy&rdquo;&mdash;not adding AI onto existing businesses, but restructuring entirely around it. For individuals, his message was simple: &ldquo;Just start using it.&rdquo;

&ldquo;AI is not something to fear. It is a tool that expands human capability,&rdquo; he concluded. &ldquo;Ultimately, competitiveness lies not in the technology itself, but in how reliably it is designed.&rdquo;
